WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.760
A quarter century ago, British Telecom
reported nine nines of service

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.640
availability in one of its telephone
switching systems.

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.020
That's equivalent to about 30 milliseconds
of downtime a year.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.400
They achieved that with components
engineers knew would fail built with

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.220
Erlang and its open telecom platform OTP.

6
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.040
The system used redundancy and recovery to
contain those component failures and

7
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.460
keep service running. That's the question
behind this paper.

8
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.960
Can OTP help AI systems succeed reliably
despite component failures?

9
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.460
Today's agents can crash loop, drift off
task, or hallucinate.

10
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.640
With tool access, they can make
inappropriate or dangerous tool calls,

11
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.820
read API keys and private data outside
their authorized task, or exfiltrate

12
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.340
data. Security evaluations repeatedly
demonstrate tool misuse and data leakage,

13
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.940
including successful attacks against
systems with defenses.

14
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.040
We want whole systems that coordinate many
workers, enforce what each can read

15
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.640
and do and keep useful work moving when
individual agents break in any of these

16
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.000
ways or in ways we haven't anticipated. On
our test tasks, OTP-style

17
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.560
supervised configurations delivered
substantial gains in reliable task

18
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.200
completion with the same language model.
In two selected internal comparisons,

19
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.200
frontier model configurations incurred
roughly 4 to 20 times as much in language

20
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.560
model charges per successfully completed
task run as smaller model

21
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.280
configurations. That calculation includes
failed attempts and recovery.

22
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.280
Success rates differed, so these
observations do not establish savings at

23
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.960
equal quality or cover the full cost of
operating the system.

24
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.040
Our mapping shows which patterns transfer
and where the engineering must change.

25
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.760
It points to the work ahead, extending
those capability gains and building

26
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.940
toward telecom-style availability. I'm
Mick Darling, founder of Dollhouse

27
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.620
Research, an AI lab. We build platform
agnostic tools that make AI more

28
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.820
capable and give people the visibility and
control to govern its behavior.

29
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.440
I'm the lead inventor on four natural
language processing patents.

30
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:06.840
That work began in the early 2010s using
Erlang OTP workers to analyze the

31
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.860
meaning of unstructured social media text
in real time. We processed millions of

32
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.080
messages for live television, including
American Idol. That experience helped us

33
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.280
recognize familiar engineering problems in
multi-agent systems.

34
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.420
I also want to thank the chairs,
reviewers,

35
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.180
and proceedings team for helping bring
this paper to publication and for their

36
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.900
patience with me along the way.

37
00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.860
They've inspired a new agentic benchmark
navigating the publication process

38
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.520
without asking the chairs for help. I can
report that the human baseline

39
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.460
required considerable assistance. An agent
that could do it unaided would be a

40
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.100
remarkable uplift in at least this human's
capability.

41
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.380
The immediate problem in our production
system was waiting.

42
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.320
It arose in DollhouseMCP, an MCP server we
use for agent management.

43
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.100
In the setup I'm describing, it runs
locally.

44
00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.740
There are four distinct parts here. The
model generates responses.

45
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.580
The harness is the application that calls
the model and coordinates tool use.

46
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.880
MCP, the model context protocol, lets that
application access tools exposed by a

47
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.880
server. Our agent is the managed unit of
work with an identity, state, and a

48
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.000
mailbox for messages. Its lifetime can
span several model calls.

49
00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.040
Some agents needed to wait hours for an
outside event. One unattended component

50
00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.220
repeatedly called on the model to check
for updates and consumed most of a

51
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.840
weekly quota. An open idle session doesn't
inherently consume tokens.

52
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.620
Those repeated inference calls do. We
moved responsibility for waiting into the

53
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.700
server. Ordinary code retains the agent's
identity and messages without keeping

54
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.560
a model call active or awaiting its
completion.

55
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.560
We call that parking. It uses computing
resources but no inference tokens while

56
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.220
parked. An event, timer, or server side
result can mark the agent as

57
00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:00.340
ready to continue. A client or dispatcher
must then arrange the next model call.

58
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.620
How that happens depends on the
deployment. Retaining state does not by

59
00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.080
itself provide the whole wake up
mechanism. A harness can also wait for

60
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.640
events without repeatedly calling the
model. We put lifecycle state in the

61
00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.540
server to separate the agent's lifetime
from individual model interactions.

62
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.260
Routing tool requests through that server
also gives us a place to apply access

63
00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.680
controls before those tools execute. Once
ordinary code controls when an agent

64
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.120
starts, waits, and stops, it can also
monitor work, start a fresh model call

65
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.460
after a failure, and limit how often that
happens. That's supervision.

66
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.640
In OTP, processes have identities and
mailboxes.

67
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.640
Supervisors watch workers and apply
recovery rules after a failure.

68
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.780
In April 2026, we recorded how these OTP
constructs corresponded to our

69
00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:55.760
runtime in dated engineering issues. We
deliberately applied the patterns,

70
00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.400
then ran the planned experiments in July.

71
00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.440
Could OTP help us predict failures and
useful fixes in agent systems?

72
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.920
We first mapped 20 OTP constructs to their
proposed agent counterparts, judging

73
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.260
how faithfully the behavior transfers. We
then ran pre-registered experiments,

74
00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.400
comparisons, and analysis rules set before
running the models.

75
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:23.080
Those compared supervised configurations
with simpler loops across model tiers.

76
00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.920
Finally, we examined the differences that
prevent a faithful mapping.

77
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.380
Production experience motivated the
investigation. The mapping and experiments

78
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.160
test different claims, and neither
requires agent frameworks to be

79
00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:39.336
implemented in Erlang. Of the 20 OTP
constructs, twelve had exact operational

80
00:05:39.336 --> 00:05:42.916
mappings their proposed agent counterparts
preserve all the operational behavior

81
00:05:42.916 --> 00:05:44.396
relevant to the comparison.

82
00:05:45.296 --> 00:05:48.836
Five mapped more loosely, the general
function carries over, but documented

83
00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:52.476
differences prevent a fully faithful
mapping. For three, the mapping breaks

84
00:05:52.476 --> 00:05:55.876
because the agent infrastructure we
examined offers no faithful counterpart.

85
00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.300
Those grades describe behavior, not just
resemblance in

86
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.400
names. Take parking, one of the exact
mappings.

87
00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.440
An Erlang process waiting for a message
retains its identity and mailbox without

88
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.740
doing work. Our parked agent also retains
an addressable identity and mailbox

89
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:19.880
without keeping a model call open. The
resources differ, but both separate the

90
00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.900
worker's continuing existence from its
current activity.

91
00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:27.680
Calling that mapping exact does not
certify every implementation or give an

92
00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.540
agent every guarantee of an Erlang
process.

93
00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:34.120
We assign the grades ourselves, and they
have not been independently audited.

94
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.100
The dated record and evidence for each row
let others inspect and challenge

95
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.700
those judgments. Two of the examples in
this table show how the mapping guided

96
00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:48.260
repairs. A supervisor also needs a limit
on restarts, otherwise it can keep

97
00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:52.680
restarting a failing worker indefinitely.
After that limit, it needs to escalate

98
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.580
the failure rather than retry forever.
Workers also need a defined relationship

99
00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:02.060
to their client session. Some of ours kept
running for days after their sessions

100
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:07.360
died. Linking those lifetimes gives the
system a rule for stopping work when its

101
00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.100
owning session ends. These were fixes for
DollhouseMCP.

102
00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.300
We recorded the designs in April before
the matching incidents appeared.

103
00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:19.100
As the paper notes, our runtime’s
designers already knew Erlang.

104
00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.520
This record shows us applying prior
knowledge. It motivates the mapping

105
00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.780
without independently auditing it. The
three constructs without faithful

106
00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.720
counterparts show where the underlying
infrastructure matters.

107
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.840
First, Erlang processes are isolated from
one another by default.

108
00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:39.200
LLM systems often accumulate observations
and contexts, and some share a growing

109
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.260
history. We can give workers fresh context
as an isolation policy that does not

110
00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.480
provide the same built-in guarantee as
separate Erlang process memory.

111
00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.980
Second, an Erlang process checks
structured message values against expected

112
00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:55.640
patterns. A model interprets the content
it receives, including prose inside an

113
00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:00.380
otherwise valid message. That prose can
contain an instruction or an injection

114
00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:04.460
attack. Checking the message's structure
does not establish that its meaning is

115
00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:09.260
safe. Third, Erlang can interrupt a
process during execution and let other

116
00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:13.600
processes run. The hosted model interfaces
available to us did not expose

117
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:17.860
comparable control inside an inference
call. We could monitor calls, impose

118
00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.780
timeouts, and control what happened
between calls. That is a limit of the

119
00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:25.360
interfaces we evaluated, not a claim that
model inference can never become

120
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:28.080
interruptible. These are substantive
differences.

121
00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:32.220
Organizing agents into a supervision tree
does not automatically give them all

122
00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.340
of Erlang's guarantees.

123
00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:38.860
To test a configuration, we built a
standalone research harness separate from

124
00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:40.660
the production MCP deployment.

125
00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:46.400
It assigns work to LLM workers and checks
their messages and outputs.

126
00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:51.240
The supervisor responds to reported
failures. A separate experimental grader

127
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:56.180
scores answer correctness. Supervisors,
routers, validators, and mailboxes are

128
00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.020
ordinary code. One worker activation is
one model call.

129
00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:04.180
After a failure, a replacement receives
its assigned work in a fresh context

130
00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.820
without the failed call's accumulated
history.

131
00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.860
Task state is retained outside that
context. The persistent production agent

132
00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.740
we parked earlier is distinct from these
individual model calls.

133
00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.280
When we report actor count, we mean the
configured LLM workers excluding control

134
00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.080
code and additional calls made for
recovery. The supervisor can detect

135
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.980
crashes, timeouts, invalid message
structure, and outputs that fail the

136
00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.200
configured requirements. It can replace a
failed activation, hold a suspect

137
00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.900
message aside, or escalate after the
restart limit.

138
00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:40.240
A confident wrong answer that passes those
checks may produce no failure signal.

139
00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.420
The first study compares three
configurations.

140
00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:50.140
A is the supervised system. B is a simpler
loop with a fixed retry

141
00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:54.660
policy that retains prior context. C makes
no retries.

142
00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:00.560
A later comparison adds S, a control that
generates multiple candidate answers.

143
00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:05.400
A combines several design choices so these
comparisons cannot identify fresh

144
00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.440
context alone as the cause of improvement.
Workers run sequentially.

145
00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.860
The experiments establish no concurrency
latency or throughput advantage.

146
00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:19.180
We tested eight model tiers from models
with 1.7 billion parameters

147
00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:24.800
to frontier models. The first study uses
multistep tasks in the

148
00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.620
style of the GAIA benchmark. We check
whether each final answer exactly matches

149
00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.940
the expected answer after applying
predefined rules for how answers are

150
00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:36.300
written. A plausible intermediate step
does not count as success.

151
00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.260
Each task gets four complete runs under
each configuration.

152
00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:45.280
A run may include its permitted retries,
but those retries do not count as

153
00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:50.120
additional runs. Our reliability metric,
pass to the fourth, counts a task as

154
00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.180
successful only if all four runs produce
correct final answers.

155
00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.480
We analyze those task level results rather
than treating retries as independent

156
00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.380
observations. The later scaling study uses
harder problems that can be divided

157
00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:06.420
into components. Workers follow chains of
proof, trace where information came

158
00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.420
from, compare possible fault causes, or
allocate resources under constraints.

159
00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.980
These represent aspects of software
engineering work rather than an end-to-end

160
00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:19.260
coding benchmark. We set acceptance
conditions for each conclusion before

161
00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.540
running the models. When a condition
failed, we withheld that conclusion, even

162
00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:27.940
if other measurements remained usable.
Each result here reports the proportion

163
00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:32.280
of 20 capability tasks that succeeded on
all four complete runs.

164
00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:36.600
With Ministral-3B, the supervised
configuration reaches 95%.

165
00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:40.840
Both the retry loop and the no-retry loop
reach 65%.

166
00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:46.380
With Qwen3-32B, the supervised
configuration reaches 70% compared with

167
00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:50.400
45% for the retry loop and 5% without
retries.

168
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.020
Retries alone clearly help. Supervision
adds 25 percentage points beyond that

169
00:11:56.020 --> 00:12:00.040
retry control. That improvement belongs to
the whole supervised configuration.

170
00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:02.900
We did not separately test how much comes
from fresh context.

171
00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.440
Across the five tiers where results
distinguish the configurations,

172
00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.980
supervision leads both controls at four
and ties the stronger control at one.

173
00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:16.560
The frontier models reach 100% under every
configuration while the smallest

174
00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.780
model reaches zero. Those results cannot
show an improvement on this task suite.

175
00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:24.420
With only 20 tasks per result, this is a
pilot study.

176
00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:28.860
We make no statistical significance claim
for an individual result.

177
00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:32.240
These models also differ in more than
parameter count.

178
00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:37.260
So our defensible comparisons use the same
model under different configurations.

179
00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.340
Could the improvement simply come from
generating more attempts?

180
00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:46.720
We tested a best-of-N control that runs
the simpler unsupervised loop to

181
00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:51.000
generate candidate answers then selects
one. The selector checks the required

182
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.740
format and uses agreement when several
candidates fit the budget otherwise it

183
00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:59.540
takes the first valid answer. It cannot
see correct answers or grading results.

184
00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:04.580
We compared configurations on the same 20
tasks initially across three model

185
00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:09.940
tiers then with fresh runs at 32B the
initial comparison averages across tiers.

186
00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:16.580
Success means a correct individual run
rather than requiring all four

187
00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:21.360
runs of a task to succeed. Supervision's
advantages are about 33 percentage

188
00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:26.620
points in the initial study and 26 in the
replication with p-values at or below

189
00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:32.200
0.004. Across 320 sampling control task
runs,

190
00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:36.540
the selector never turned a failed first
candidate into a successful selected

191
00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:39.900
answer. This concerns the tested selector
and sampling schedule.

192
00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:43.500
It does not rule out stronger sampling
methods or identify which feature of

193
00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:48.340
supervision caused the advantage. Both
studies aim to keep sampling token

194
00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:52.020
spending within 10% of supervision
spending.

195
00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:55.060
Both miss that band in opposite
directions.

196
00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:59.540
We therefore have evidence of an advantage
but cannot claim it was established

197
00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:04.140
with matched computational resources. A
stronger comparison must enforce the

198
00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:07.160
budget without using outcomes to decide
how much to spend.

199
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.320
Can dividing work among supervised workers
help on harder problems?

200
00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:16.680
Here the apparatus supplies 16 component
problems and a plan for assigning them.

201
00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:21.480
With one worker a single model call
answers the whole batch with 16 workers

202
00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.960
each answers one component. Ordinary code
assembles the results and workers run

203
00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:30.500
sequentially. We test execution of the
supplied plan rather than asking models

204
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:35.620
to invent the decomposition. We now
measure component accuracy the proportion

205
00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:37.460
of components answered correctly.

206
00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:42.540
Missing or incomplete output counts as
failure. That is different from requiring

207
00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:48.160
an entire task to succeed. The control
code stays fixed as worker count changes.

208
00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:54.680
On tasks reserved in advance for
evaluation, Qwen3-32B rises from

209
00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:59.860
57.4% with one worker to 81.6% with 16.

210
00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:05.820
That’s a gain of 24.2 percentage points
with a 95% confidence interval from

211
00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:09.260
13.3 to 35.5.

212
00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:14.900
Repeating the experiment in a separately
provisioned setup yields 53.5% and

213
00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:20.660
80.9%. Haiku 4.5 instead peaks at four
workers, at

214
00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:25.120
54.7%, then falls to 34.0% with 16.

215
00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.600
Worker count therefore needs to suit the
model and task.

216
00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:33.780
In the paper a controller that adjusts
worker count during execution is proposed

217
00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:36.880
future work. The paper does not report a
test of one.

218
00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:40.980
That Qwen reproduction failed the
requirements for matched resource

219
00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:44.520
spending. We can report its accuracy but
not matched cost savings.

220
00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.440
We separately exclude Sonnet 5’s apparent
gains.

221
00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:51.080
Dividing its batch repairs a JSON output
format problem rather than

222
00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:55.620
demonstrating better reasoning. This
leaves research questions beyond the three

223
00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.220
constructs that failed to map.

224
00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:01.960
First, how do we recognize an answer that
is wrong in meaning?

225
00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:06.820
A worker can return a confident
well-formed answer that passes validation.

226
00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:10.900
The supervisor then sees no reason to
restart it. Recovery needs an observable

227
00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:16.500
failure signal. Second, fresh contexts
limit what each worker receives, but they

228
00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:20.500
are only part of our isolation policy. A
message permitted through an allowed

229
00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:23.700
path can still carry instructions that
influence subsequent work.

230
00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:27.220
Fresh context alone does not make those
messages trustworthy.

231
00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:33.640
Third, recovery has a cost. Each fresh
model call consumes tokens, time, and

232
00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:34.190
money.

233
00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:39.460
Restarting is not automatically worth the
expense. In the paper we did not

234
00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:41.960
establish the matched cost claims.

235
00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:47.380
OTP supplies useful defaults but applying
them still requires understanding the

236
00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:52.360
model and failures we can detect. There
are two judgments here.

237
00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:57.800
How faithfully an OTP pattern maps to an
agent system and how much evidence we

238
00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:02.100
have for its implementation. An exact
mapping describes the operational

239
00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:06.460
correspondence. It does not certify a
complete implementation or tell us how

240
00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:09.100
much one mechanism contributed to the
measured gain.

241
00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:12.800
The paper separates implementation
evidence into three levels.

242
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:17.200
Tested means sealed preregistered evidence
including the integrated supervision

243
00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.480
and decomposition studies we've just seen.

244
00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:24.980
Spot means validation or exploratory
evidence. For example, the deterministic

245
00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:28.200
injected fault test produced all 18
expected outcomes.

246
00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:31.620
That checks those recovery cases but does
not establish a comparative capability

247
00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:32.860
gain under injected faults.

248
00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:37.460
Gap means a design with no experimental
result in the paper.

249
00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.340
Continuous feedback control and
heterogeneous fleets are examples.

250
00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:45.420
Keeping these evidence levels separate
lets us distinguish a useful

251
00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.860
architectural correspondence from a result
we have actually measured.

252
00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:53.640
The paper proposes three next tests. These
are research questions not completed

253
00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:54.860
confirmatory results.

254
00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:59.680
First, compare supervision with the retry
loop on a paid grid of deliberately

255
00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:04.360
injected faults. Does adding those faults
widen the supervision advantage?

256
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.800
The deterministic self-tests on the
previous slide do not answer that

257
00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:09.860
comparative question.

258
00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:11.210
Second,

259
00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:16.080
test whether a supervised small model
configuration can match a clearly defined

260
00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:21.120
frontier baseline on the same suite and
measure the corresponding cost ratio.

261
00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:26.580
The pilot's cross model observations
motivate that question but do not

262
00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:28.820
establish parity at equal quality.

263
00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:32.840
Third, sweep actor counts across models
and task families.

264
00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:36.560
Can we predict which level of
decomposition suits a given model and

265
00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:40.280
task, instead of assuming that more
workers always help?

266
00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:44.580
Each conclusion must meet its
pre-registered acceptance conditions.

267
00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:48.500
The paper's proposed tests remain distinct
from the separately labeled research

268
00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:53.260
updates in the backup slides. The unit we
measured is the configured system, the

269
00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.920
model together with supervision validators
and retained task state.

270
00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:02.820
On the pilot tasks, Ministral-3B reached
95 percent reliable task completion

271
00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:06.900
with supervision compared with 65 percent
without retries.

272
00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:10.460
Reliable here means that a task passed all
four complete runs.

273
00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:15.920
On the harder decomposable tasks,
Qwen3-32B’s component accuracy rose from

274
00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:21.440
57.4 percent with one worker to 81.6
percent with 16 using the plan supplied by

275
00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:22.060
the apparatus.

276
00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:28.280
Haiku instead peaked at four workers and
fell to 34.0 percent at 16.

277
00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:31.080
Worker count must suit the model and the
task.

278
00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:37.240
These results show why we need to evaluate
the system managing a model's work as

279
00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:41.440
well as the model itself. The paper DOI is
on the slide.

280
00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:44.200
Thank you. I'm happy to take questions.
